WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.490
Last year, a pupil found two of

2
00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:05.830
his exams in the same slot.

3
00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:08.110
He sat one of them in the

4
00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:09.390
staff room at lunchtime.

5
00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:13.530
This year I want no clashes, and

6
00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.410
as few slots as possible.

7
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.820
Today: exams without clashes, as

8
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:21.540
a picture and as a model.

9
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:24.820
Draw each exam as a dot.

10
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:28.120
Join two dots when some pupil takes both

11
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:30.800
exams: those two can’t share a slot.

12
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.020
Now giving exams slots is

13
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.300
colouring the dots, so that

14
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.420
joined dots get different colours.

15
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.880
A quick rule: go down the list, and give

16
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.560
each exam the first slot with no clash.

17
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.800
In the lab, that needs four slots.

18
00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:52.720
A smarter rule, DSatur, always

19
00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.020
does next the exam that is hardest

20
00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.000
to place, and needs three.

21
00:00:58.220 --> 00:00:59.220
Can it be done in two?

22
00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:00.070
No.

23
00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.820
Omar’s three exams, maths, Arabic and

24
00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.600
French, all clash with each other, so

25
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:07.960
they need three different slots.

26
00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.570
A group of exams that all clash is

27
00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:13.810
called a clique, and it proves

28
00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:15.470
that fewer is impossible.

29
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:20.300
In CP-SAT, each exam’s slot is a variable.

30
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.460
For every pupil, their exams must

31
00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.100
all be in different slots.

32
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.600
A variable called last is at least

33
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.180
every slot, and the goal is to make

34
00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.560
it as small as possible.

35
00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:35.810
The solver answers three,

36
00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:37.750
and says OPTIMAL.

37
00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:40.890
That word matters: the solver has

38
00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.270
proved that no plan with two slots

39
00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.910
exists, just as Omar’s clique said.

40
00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:50.150
A quick rule can only say “this works”; a

41
00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.910
solver can say “this is the best”.

42
00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:54.470
Quick check!

43
00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:58.540
A quick rule found four slots, and the

44
00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.980
solver says OPTIMAL with three.

45
00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.650
What does the word OPTIMAL add?

46
00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:09.350
A proof: no plan with fewer slots exists.

47
00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:13.260
But look at Omar and Tarek: with three

48
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.240
slots, they sit three exams back to back.

49
00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:18.710
Add the rule “no pupil sits

50
00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:21.110
exams in two slots in a row”,

51
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:22.550
and the fewest becomes five.

52
00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.380
Fewer slots, or rested

53
00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.300
pupils: that’s a trade-off.

54
00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:30.550
Who should decide that trade-off?

55
00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.200
Not the solver, and not

56
00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.780
one timetabler alone.

57
00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:38.580
Ask the pupils who sit the exams and

58
00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.240
the teachers who mark them, then write

59
00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.340
the agreed rule into the model.

60
00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.460
In the app, open “Exam

61
00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:48.100
slots without clashes”.

62
00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.080
Press Greedy, then DSatur,

63
00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.160
then “How few slots?”.

64
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.220
Then untick Omar, and

65
00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:57.240
colour the map yourself.

66
00:02:58.550 --> 00:02:59.290
Your turn.

67
00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:03.990
Our model says three slots, OPTIMAL, but

68
00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.710
Tarek has three exams back to back.

69
00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.580
What should we do?

70
00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:11.670
Agree the rule with the people it

71
00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:14.610
affects, add it, solve again, and

72
00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.130
say openly what it costs.

73
00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:20.490
Remember: exams are dots, shared pupils

74
00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.190
are lines, and slots are colours.

75
00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:25.990
A clique proves the fewest.

76
00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:29.750
And OPTIMAL means best for the goal you

77
00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.190
wrote, not best for every pupil.

78
00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.080
Try it in class tomorrow: list next

79
00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.880
term’s exams, and let pupils draw the

80
00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.800
clash map with chalk or string.

81
00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:44.050
Colour it with as few slots as you can.

82
00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.460
Then ask: who has two exams in

83
00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.520
a row, and is that fair?
